\setcounter{theorem}{27}\begin{lemma}[Completeness]\label{cd7:complete}
The space $(S,\dist_S)$ is complete. Given any $z_0,z_1\in S$, there is a path $p$ with $\operatorname{length}(p)=\dist_S(z_0,z_1)$; in other words, there is at least one geodesic.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
First take $S=\mathbb D$. We may assume $z_0=0$ and $z_1=r\in\mathbb R$, with $0<r<1$, by applying an automorphism of $\mathbb D$. Let $p\colon[0,1]\to\mathbb D$ join $0$ to $r$, and write $p(t)=(x(t),y(t))$. Then
\[
\sqrt{\frac4{(1-|p(t)|^2)^2}\bigl(dx^2(p',p')+dy^2(p',p')\bigr)}
\ge\sqrt{\frac4{(1-x(t)^2)^2}\,dx^2(p',p')}.
\]
Thus
\[
\int_pds=\int_p\frac{2|dz|}{1-|z|^2}
\ge\int_p\frac{2|dx|}{1-x^2}
\ge\int_0^r\frac{2\,dx}{1-x^2}
=\log\frac{1+r}{1-r}.
\]
Equality holds if and only if $y(t)=0$. If $\psi$ takes $z_0,z_1$ to $0,r$, then
\[
\dist_{\mathbb D}(z_0,z_1)
=\dist_{\mathbb D}(\psi(z_0),\psi(z_1))
=\log\frac{1+|\psi(z_1)|}{1-|\psi(z_1)|}.
\]
Hyperbolic balls in $\mathbb D$ are Euclidean balls, generally with different centres. Thus on $\widetilde S=\overline{B_r(0)}$, $r<1$, the hyperbolic and Euclidean metrics give the same topology. In particular, the boundary of $\mathbb D$ is at infinite Poincar\'e distance. A Poincar\'e-Cauchy sequence therefore stays in a compact Euclidean subdisc, where the Euclidean and Poincar\'e metrics induce the same topology; it consequently converges in $\mathbb D$. Geodesics exist and are uniquely determined by two distinct points.
% Source page 8
Now let $S$ be any hyperbolic surface. For small enough $\varepsilon$, the set $\{z\in S:\dist_S(z,z_0)\le\varepsilon\}$ is compact. In fact, if $P\colon\mathbb D\to S$ is the covering map with $P(0)=z_0$, then the restriction of $P$ over a sufficiently small ball is an isometry. Also
\[
\dist_S(P(z_0),P(z_1))\le\dist_{\mathbb D}(z_0,z_1),
\]
since a path in $\mathbb D$ of length $\ell$ projects to a path of the same length. A closed metric ball of radius $r$ is compact, being a closed subset of a sufficiently large compact ball. For a Cauchy sequence $\{x_n\}$, there is $N$ such that its tail lies in $\{z:\dist_S(x_N,z)\le\varepsilon\}$. It therefore converges. Finally, the Hopf--Rinow theorem for length spaces gives a length-minimising path between every two points.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Heine--Borel holds for hyperbolic spaces: $N_r(z_0)=\{z\in S:\dist_S(z,z_0)\le r\}$ is compact for every $r>0$.
\end{remark}
An explicit geodesic construction is sketched below. Let $B$ be a ball of local isometry about $z_0$, and choose a path $p$ to $z_1$ with $\operatorname{length}(p)=\dist(z_0,z_1)+\varepsilon$, which is possible by the definition of the infimum.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.85\textwidth]{cd7-fig-03.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD7.3}{fig:cd7-03}
\end{figure}
Consider paths $p_j'$ with lengths $\dist(z_0,z_1)+\varepsilon_j'$, where $\varepsilon_j'\le\varepsilon$ and $\varepsilon_j'\to0$. If they cross $\partial B$ at $x_j$, choose an accumulation point $x$. Join $z_0$ to $x$ by a geodesic, then repeat.
% Source page 9

We care about hyperbolic surfaces because the other surfaces are well known: $\widehat{\mathbb C}$, $\mathbb C$, $S^1\times S^1$, and $S^1\times\mathbb R$. This follows by checking fixed-point-free automorphisms of $\widehat{\mathbb C}$ and discrete subgroups of $\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb C)$; their quotients give all spherical and Euclidean surfaces. They all have abelian fundamental group. Thus a surface with nonabelian fundamental group is immediately hyperbolic: hyperbolic surfaces abound.
\setcounter{theorem}{28}\begin{lemma}\label{cd7:three-punctures}
The surface $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\{p,q,r\}$ is hyperbolic.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is a Riemann surface with nonabelian fundamental group:
$\pi_1(\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\{p,q,r\})\cong\pi_1(\mathbb C\setminus\{p,q\})\cong\mathbb Z*\mathbb Z$.
\end{proof}
\setcounter{theorem}{29}\begin{corollary}[Picard's theorem]
If a holomorphic map $f\colon\mathbb C\to\mathbb C$ omits two distinct values, it is constant.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If $f$ omits $a,b$, it maps $\mathbb C$ to the hyperbolic surface $\mathbb C\setminus\{a,b\}$. It lifts to $\widetilde f\colon\mathbb C\to\mathbb D$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.40\textwidth]{cd7-fig-04.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD7.4}{fig:cd7-04}
\end{figure}
Liouville's theorem makes $\widetilde f$ constant. Here we regard $\mathbb D\subset\mathbb C$ without giving it its Poincar\'e metric.
\end{proof}
% Source page 10

Hyperbolic surfaces matter in complex dynamics because they help identify spaces on which a family of functions is stable. More precisely:
\setcounter{theorem}{31}\begin{lemma}
If $T$ is a hyperbolic Riemann surface, every sequence in $\Hol(S,T)$ has a subsequence that either converges locally uniformly or diverges compactly.
\end{lemma}
This normal-family property is central to complex dynamics.
\begin{lemma}[Ball convergence]
Suppose $S\subset S'$ are Riemann surfaces with $S$ hyperbolic, and let $\{p_n\}_n$ converge in $S'$ to a boundary point $\widehat p\in\partial S$. For every fixed $r>0$, the hyperbolic balls of radius $r$ centred at $p_n$ converge uniformly to $\widehat p$.
\end{lemma}

Given a rational map $f\colon\widehat{\mathbb C}\to\widehat{\mathbb C}$, one studies the iterates of $f$ \cite{milnor2011dynamics}. It is true that the poles and zeros of a rational map determine the nature of $f$ to a large extent, in the following sense.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.70\textwidth]{cd1-fig-01.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD1.1}{fig:cd1-01}
\end{figure}
Let dots denote zeros and crosses denote poles. Then $f$ has a power-series expansion in a disc with radius as large as the distance to the pole nearest the centre. But if one wants to study iterates of $f$, one must look at properties preserved by a change of coordinates:
\[
\varphi\circ f\circ\varphi^{-1}\sim f\quad\text{(dynamically)},\qquad
\varphi\circ f^{\circ n}\circ\varphi^{-1}
=(\varphi\circ f\circ\varphi^{-1})^{\circ n}\sim f^{\circ n}.
\]
Zeros and poles are, after all, two points on $\widehat{\mathbb C}$: change coordinates via a M\"obius transformation, and $0,\infty$ go to other points.
% Source page 2

Thus one must look for an invariant of $f$ that is preserved even after a suitable change of coordinates.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.31\textwidth]{cd1-fig-02.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD1.2}{fig:cd1-02}
\end{figure}
Here $\varphi$ changes coordinates, i.e. it biholomorphically changes the base space. One naturally expects the dynamics to be preserved. Investigation along these lines leads us to two natural invariants: fixed points and critical points.

Motivations aside, when we say that we want to study the dynamics of $f$ on $S$, we essentially want to study the structure of the Fatou and Julia sets of $f$. We propose to do this by studying the invariants mentioned above.
\setcounter{definition}{2}\begin{definition}[Classification of fixed points]
A fixed point $f(\widehat p)=\widehat p$ is
\begin{itemize}
\item attracting if $|f'(\widehat p)|<1$;
\item repelling if $|f'(\widehat p)|>1$;
\item indifferent if $|f'(\widehat p)|=1$.
\end{itemize}
Here $f'(\widehat p)$ means the derivative of the coordinate function of $f$.
\end{definition}
There is a natural dilemma at this juncture. The dynamics of $f$ and $f^{\circ n}$ must be the same, for if $\{f^{\circ m}|_U\}_m$ is normal, so is $\{f^{\circ mn}|_U\}_m$. Should we merely focus our study on the fixed points and critical points of $f$, or should we pick a suitable iterate $f^{\circ10000}$ and study its critical and fixed points? The following lemmas will help clear this dilemma.
\setcounter{theorem}{0}\begin{lemma}[Invariance lemma]\label{cd1:invariance}
For a rational map $f$ of degree at least two, $z\in J(f)$ if and only if $f(z)\in J(f)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is enough to show $z\in F(f)\Longleftrightarrow f(z)\in F(f)$. We first record that postcomposition with $f$ preserves normality. If $\{f^{\circ n}|_U\}_n$ is normal, it is contained in a compact set $K\subset\Hol(U,S)$.
% Source page 4
Notice that $f\circ K$ is compact in $\Hol(U,S)$. For if $C\subseteq S$ is compact and $\{g_k\}_k\subset K$ converges uniformly on $C$, then, since $f$ is uniformly continuous on compact sets, an estimate $|g_{k_0}(x)-g(x)|<\varepsilon$ for every $k_0\ge N$ and $x\in C$ ensures, after increasing $N$ if necessary, that
\[
|f\circ g_{k_0'}(x)-f\circ g(x)|<\varepsilon
\quad(k_0'\ge N',\ x\in C).
\]
Indeed, $f$ is uniformly continuous on the compact set $\overline{B_\varepsilon(g(C))}$. Thus $\{f^{\circ(n+1)}|_U\}_n$ is normal.

If $f(z)\in F(f)$, there is a neighbourhood $V$ of $f(z)$ on which $\{f^{\circ n}|_V\}_n$ is normal. Since $f$ is open, there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $z$ with $f(U)\subseteq V$. Hence $\{f^{\circ(n+1)}|_U\}_n$ is normal, and adding the single map $\id|_U$ shows that $z\in F(f)$.

Conversely, suppose $z\in F(f)$. After shrinking a neighbourhood $U$ of $z$, the map $f\colon U\to V=f(U)$ is a proper holomorphic map of finite degree. Normality descends through such a map: if $\{h_n\circ f\}_n$ is normal on $U$, then $\{h_n\}_n$ is normal on $V$. Applying this to $h_n=f^{\circ n}$ shows that $\{f^{\circ n}|_V\}_n$ is normal. Hence $f(z)\in F(f)$.
\end{proof}
The Fatou and Julia sets are invariant under orbits. The next lemma makes this more explicit.
\setcounter{theorem}{1}\begin{lemma}\label{cd1:iterate}
$J(f^{\circ n})=J(f)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By the previous lemma, it suffices to show $F(f^{\circ n})=F(f)$. Certainly $z\in F(f)$ implies $z\in F(f^{\circ n})$. If $z\in F(f^{\circ n})$, there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $z$ such that $\{f^{\circ nm}|_U\}_m\subset K\subseteq\Hol(U,S)$, with $K$ compact. Then
\[
\{f^{\circ m}|_U\}_m\subseteq\bigcup_{i=0}^{n-1}(f^{\circ i}\circ K)
\subseteq\Hol(U,S),
\]
and this finite union is again compact.
\end{proof}
% Source page 5

\subsection{Attraction, repulsion, and indifference}
\paragraph{Attraction: multiplier $|\lambda|<1$.}
This is usually called topological attraction. Let us first state explicitly the intuitive idea of attraction to a point $\widehat p$. A point $\widehat p\in S$ is said to be attracting if there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $\widehat p$ such that $\{f^{\circ n}|_U\}_n$ converges locally uniformly on $U$ to the constant map $z\mapsto\widehat p$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.40\textwidth]{cd1-fig-03.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD1.3}{fig:cd1-03}
\end{figure}
\setcounter{theorem}{2}\begin{lemma}\label{cd1:attraction}
Let $f(\widehat p)=\widehat p$, where $f\in\Hol(S,S)$. Then $|f'(\widehat p)|<1$ if and only if $\widehat p$ is attracting.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $\widehat f$ be the coordinate expression of $f$ locally at $\widehat p$, centred at $\widehat p$. For simplicity assume $\widehat f=f$. If $f(0)=0$ and $|\lambda|=|f'(0)|<1$, analyticity gives $f(z)=\lambda z+O(z^2)$. Thus there is $r_0>0$ such that $|f(z)-\lambda z|=O(|z|^2)$ for $z\in\mathbb D_{r_0}$, and
\[
|f(z)|\le |\lambda z|+C|z|^2\le (|\lambda|+C|z|)|z|.
\]
If $z\in\mathbb D_{r_0}$ is small enough that $|\lambda|+C|z|<1$, then $f$ becomes a contraction mapping, proving that $0$ is attracting.
% Source page 7
One can choose $|\lambda|<c<1$ and $0<r\le r_0$ with $|\lambda|+Cr<c$. In particular, $|\lambda|+C|z|<c<1$ for all $z\in\mathbb D_r$. Hence $|f(z)|<c|z|$, $f(z)\in\mathbb D_r$, and $|f^{\circ n}(z)|<c^n|z|$, so $f^{\circ n}\to(z\mapsto0)$.

Conversely, if $\widehat p$ is attracting, there is $\varepsilon>0$ and an $n\ge1$ such that $f^{\circ n}(\mathbb D_\varepsilon)$ is relatively compact in $\mathbb D_\varepsilon$. By Schwarz's lemma, $|(f^{\circ n})'(0)|<1$, so $|f'(0)|<1$.
\end{proof}
Notice that we need holomorphicity of $f$ to conclude that $|\lambda|<1$ is geometrically attracting.

\paragraph{Repulsion: multiplier $|\lambda|>1$.}
This is usually called topological repulsion. Let $f(\widehat p)=\widehat p$. The point $\widehat p$ is repelling if there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $\widehat p$ such that, for every $p\in U\setminus\{\widehat p\}$, there is $N_p$ with $f^{\circ n}(p)\notin U$ for some $n\ge N_p$. Equivalently, $f$ is locally invertible at $\widehat p$ and $\widehat p$ is attracting for the local inverse fixing $\widehat p$. In other words, all orbits except that of $\widehat p$ eventually escape $U$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.40\textwidth]{cd1-fig-04.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD1.4}{fig:cd1-04}
\end{figure}
\setcounter{theorem}{3}\begin{lemma}\label{cd1:repulsion}
$|f'(\widehat p)|>1$ if and only if $\widehat p$ is repelling.
\end{lemma}
% Source page 8

\setcounter{theorem}{4}\begin{lemma}\label{cd1:parabolic}
Parabolic points belong to the Julia set.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Passing to an iterate, we may assume that the multiplier is $1$.
Locally, $f(w)=w+a_qw^q+a_{q+1}w^{q+1}+\cdots$, so $f^{\circ k}(w)=w+ka_qw^q+\cdots$. Consequently,
\[
\left.\frac{d^q(f^{\circ k})}{dz^q}\right|_0=q!\,ka_q\longrightarrow\infty
\qquad(k\to\infty).
\]
Thus $\{f^{\circ n}\}_n$ cannot converge locally uniformly, for then the sequence of $q$th derivatives at $0$ would also converge.
\end{proof}
Therefore attracting basins lie in the Fatou set, while repelling and parabolic points belong to the Julia set.

\setcounter{theorem}{7}\begin{proposition}[Expansion]\label{cd1:expansion}
Let $z\in J$. If $N$ is a neighbourhood of $z$, then
\[
\bigcup_{n\ge0}f^{\circ n}(N)\supseteq\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal E(f).
\]
Equality holds for sufficiently small $N$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $U=\bigcup_{n\ge0}f^{\circ n}(N)$ omits three points, note that $f(U)\subseteq U$. Since $U$ is hyperbolic, $\{f^{\circ n}|_U\}_n$ is normal, so $z\in F(f)$, a contradiction. Thus $|\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus U|\le2$. As $f(U)\subseteq U$, the points in $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus U$ must belong to the grand-orbit-finite set. Finally, equality holds if $N\subset\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal E(f)$.
\end{proof}
\setcounter{theorem}{8}\begin{proposition}[Empty interior]\label{cd1:interior}
If $\operatorname{Int}(J)\ne\varnothing$, then $J=\widehat{\mathbb C}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $\operatorname{Int}(J)\ne\varnothing$, there is a point $z$ with a neighbourhood $N\subseteq J$. By the invariance lemma, $\bigcup_n f^{\circ n}(N)\subseteq J$. Since $J$ is closed,
$J\supseteq\overline{\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal E(f)}=\widehat{\mathbb C}$, giving the assertion.
\end{proof}
% Source page 14

Recall that basins belong to the Fatou set: there open sets shrink, whereas for the Julia set, open sets expand to fill almost all of $\widehat{\mathbb C}$. Let us put these two concepts together.
\setcounter{theorem}{9}\begin{proposition}[Basin boundary equals Julia set]\label{cd1:boundary}
If $\mathcal A$ is an attracting basin, then $\partial\mathcal A=J$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The Julia set expands open sets around it. If $z\in J(f)$ and $N$ is a neighbourhood of $z$, there is $k_0$ with $f^{\circ k_0}(N)\cap\mathcal A\ne\varnothing$. By invariance, $N\cap\mathcal A\ne\varnothing$, so $J(f)\subseteq\overline{\mathcal A}$. Since $J(f)\cap\mathcal A=\varnothing$, $J(f)\subseteq\partial\mathcal A$.

Now let $z\in\partial\mathcal A$. Every neighbourhood $N$ of $z$ meets both $\mathcal A$ and $\mathcal A^c$. For $z'\in N\cap\mathcal A$, $f^{\circ n}(z')$ converges to a point, whereas this is not so for $z''\in N\cap\mathcal A^c$. This contrast is made precise by observing that $\mathcal A$ is a union of Fatou components: if one point of a Fatou component belongs to $\mathcal A$, normality and the identity theorem show that every point of that component converges to the same attracting cycle. No point of the open set $F(f)$ can therefore lie on $\partial\mathcal A$. Hence $\partial\mathcal A\subseteq J(f)$, and $\partial\mathcal A=J(f)$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
This property essentially spells out the nature of the dynamics of $f$. An attracting basin has connected components, or islands, separated by a thin set $J$ (it has no interior). The connected components of the Fatou set are called the Fatou components of $f$.
\end{remark}
% Source page 15

Understanding the dynamics near fixed points amounts to finding a change of coordinates in which the orbits of $f$ are easy to describe, as expected. A general technique is to construct a local biholomorphism $\phi$ such that the following diagram commutes.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.32\textwidth]{cd2-fig-01.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.1}{fig:cd2-01}
\end{figure}
% Source page 2

\Needspace{18\baselineskip}
\setcounter{theorem}{10}\begin{theorem}[Koenigs linearisation]\label{cd2:koenigs}
If the multiplier satisfies $|\lambda|\notin\{0,1\}$, there is a map $\phi$, unique up to multiplication by a nonzero constant, such that the following diagram commutes.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.32\textwidth]{cd2-fig-02.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.2}{fig:cd2-02}
\end{figure}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Case 1: $|\lambda|<1$.} Identify $f$ with its local coordinate expression $\widehat f$. Let $0$ be the fixed point. We have $f(z)=\lambda z+O(z^2)$ in $\mathbb D_{r_0}$ for some $r_0<1$, and
\[
|f(z)|\le|\lambda z|+C|z|^2\le(|\lambda|+C|z|)|z|\qquad(|z|<r_0).
\]
Choose $c$ with $|\lambda|<c<\sqrt{|\lambda|}$, and then choose $0<r\le r_0$ so that $|\lambda|+Cr\le c$. Then $|f(z)|\le c|z|$ for every $z\in\mathbb D_r$, so $f(z)\in\mathbb D_r$ and
$|f^{\circ n}(z)|\le c^n|z|<c^nr\to0$ as $n\to\infty$.
% Source page 4
This is precisely the push/pull we were discussing. There is $r<1$ such that $|f(z)|<c|z|$ on $\mathbb D_r$, with $c<1$. Let $\{z_n\}_n$ be the orbit of $z_0\in\mathbb D_r$. All these orbits converge to the origin, since $|z_n|\le rc^n$, and $|z_{n+1}-\lambda z_n|\le C|z_n|^2\le Cr^2c^{2n}$. Set $w_n=z_n/\lambda^n$. Then
\begin{align*}
|w_{n+1}-w_n|
&=\left|\frac{z_{n+1}}{\lambda^{n+1}}-\frac{z_n}{\lambda^n}\right|
=\frac{|z_{n+1}-\lambda z_n|}{|\lambda|^{n+1}}\\
&\le\frac{Cr^2c^{2n}}{|\lambda|^{n+1}}
=\frac{Cr^2}{|\lambda|}\left(\frac{c^2}{|\lambda|}\right)^n.
\end{align*}
The sequence $\{w_n\}_n$ is Cauchy, and its geometric bound is independent of $z_0$. The map $\phi_n(z_0)=f^{\circ n}(z_0)/\lambda^n$ is holomorphic on $\mathbb D_r$, with $\phi_n(f(z_0))=\lambda\phi_{n+1}(z_0)$. Taking $\phi=\lim_{n\to\infty}\phi_n$ gives $\phi\circ f=\lambda\phi$. Also, $\phi_n'(0)=1$, so $\phi'(0)=1$, and $\phi$ is a local conformal isomorphism around $0$.

\emph{Case 2: $|\lambda|>1$.} Since $f'(0)\ne0$, $f$ is locally invertible. Its local inverse has multiplier $0<|1/\lambda|<1$ at $0$.
% Source page 5
For uniqueness, if $\phi,\psi$ are two such Koenigs maps, then $\psi\circ\phi^{-1}$ commutes with $z\mapsto\lambda z$. If $\psi\circ\phi^{-1}(w)=\sum_{n\ge1}b_nw^n$, then $\lambda b_n=b_n\lambda^n$. Thus $b_k=0$ for every $k\ge2$, and $\psi=b_1\phi$.
\end{proof}
\subsection{Superattracting fixed points}
If $f'(0)=0$, then locally $f(z)=a_nz^n+a_{n+1}z^{n+1}+\cdots$. Thus $f$ has a zero of order $n$ at $0$, and in a suitable domain $f(z)=h(z)^nz^n$. Close to the origin in this domain, $f$ acts almost like the polynomial $a_nz^n$. The following theorem makes a much sharper claim.
\setcounter{theorem}{11}\begin{theorem}[B\"ottcher]\label{cd2:bottcher}
If $0$ is a superattracting fixed point of $f$, with a zero of order $n$ at $0$, there is a map $\phi$, unique up to multiplication by an $(n-1)$th root of unity, such that the following diagram commutes.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.32\textwidth]{cd2-fig-03.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.3}{fig:cd2-03}
\end{figure}
\end{theorem}
% Source page 6

Now that we understand the local dynamics near geometrically attracting, repelling, and superattracting points, to what extent can we extend this knowledge? In other words, on how large a domain is the nature of $f$ near these fixed points as we asserted? Let us globalise this local behaviour as far as nature allows, primarily for rational maps on $\widehat{\mathbb C}$.
\subsection{Global linearisation}
Koenigs' function $\phi$ extends holomorphically to the basin of attraction $\mathcal A$ of the fixed point $\widehat p$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.32\textwidth]{cd2-fig-04.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.4}{fig:cd2-04}
\end{figure}
\begin{proof}
The formula $\phi(f^{\circ N}(z))/\lambda^N$ is valid on $\mathcal A$, and the resulting local definitions agree on overlaps. For every $z\in\mathcal A$, there is a neighbourhood $\overline U\subseteq\mathcal A$ on which $f^{\circ n}$ converges uniformly to $\widehat p$. Thus there is $N$ such that $f^{\circ n}(z)\in U_0$, the local domain of $\phi$, for every $n\ge N$ and $z\in\overline U$. Then
% Source page 9
\[
\frac{\phi(f^{\circ N}(z))}{\lambda^N}
=\frac{\phi(f^{\circ M}(z))}{\lambda^M}\qquad(M\ge N).
\]
Indeed, $\phi$ extends to $\mathcal A$.
\end{proof}
The local inverse $\psi=\phi^{-1}$ of Koenigs' function extends to a meromorphic function on $\mathbb C$ when $\widehat p$ is a repelling fixed point.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.36\textwidth]{cd2-fig-05.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.5}{fig:cd2-05}
\end{figure}
The proof follows the same lines: drag values in $\mathbb C$ into $\mathbb D_\varepsilon$ for small $\varepsilon$, invert via $\phi^{-1}$, and compose with a suitable iterate of $f$ to make the diagram commute. Locally,
\[
\psi(w)=f^{\circ N}\left(\phi^{-1}\left(\frac w{\lambda^N}\right)\right),\qquad
f\circ\psi(w)=f^{\circ(N+1)}\left(\phi^{-1}\left(\frac w{\lambda^N}\right)\right),
\]
while $\psi\circ\lambda(w)=f^{\circ N}\bigl(\phi^{-1}(w/\lambda^{N-1})\bigr)$.
\begin{remark}
Such an extension is not possible for B\"ottcher coordinates: to make the diagram commute one must deal with $p\mapsto\sqrt[n^k]{\phi(f^{\circ k}(p))}$, but the root is not well-defined. One can, however, extend $|\phi|$, since roots in $\mathbb R_+$ are well-defined.
\end{remark}
% Source page 10

\subsection{The phenomenon of critical points: analytic aspects}
Critical points govern rational dynamics in three significant ways:
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{0}\item Critical points form the boundary beyond which Koenigs and B\"ottcher coordinates cannot be extended biholomorphically (analytic).
\setcounter{enumi}{1}\item Critical points determine the number of attracting periodic points or orbits (analytic).
\setcounter{enumi}{2}\item They determine the topological shape of the Julia set (topological; we shall see this in \BlogPost{critical-points-and-julia-set-connectivity}{another post}).
\end{enumerate}
The analysis of a rational map is essentially determined by its roots and poles, whereas in dynamics its critical points become essential. We address these phenomena one by one. Unless otherwise stated, $f$ is a rational map with $\deg(f)\ge2$, and the surface is $\widehat{\mathbb C}$.
\subsubsection{Biholomorphic extension}
\setcounter{definition}{7}\begin{definition}
Given an attracting fixed point $\widehat p$ of a map $f\colon S\to S$, let $\mathcal A_0$ denote the connected component of its basin of attraction that contains $\widehat p$.
\end{definition}
\setcounter{theorem}{12}\begin{theorem}\label{cd2:maximal}
There is a maximal $r$ such that $\psi\colon\mathbb D_r\to\mathcal A_0$ is a holomorphic inverse of the Koenigs coordinate $\phi$. The closed disc $\overline{\mathbb D_r}$ and $\psi(\overline{\mathbb D_r})$ are homeomorphic, and $\psi(\partial\mathbb D_r)$ contains a critical point of $f$.
\end{theorem}
% Source page 11

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\phi^{-1}$ extends biholomorphically to an entire function. We would then have the following commutative diagram, implying that $f|_{\psi(\mathbb C)}$ is injective.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.38\textwidth]{cd2-fig-06.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.6}{fig:cd2-06}
\end{figure}
Since $\deg(f)\ge2$, $J(f)\ne\varnothing$. By Picard's theorem, $|\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\psi(\mathbb C)|\le2$, because $\psi$ is entire. But $J(f)$ is either connected or has uncountably many components, so $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\psi(\mathbb C)$ is a single point. By invariance, $f$ is injective on $\widehat{\mathbb C}$, which is impossible when $\deg(f)\ge2$. There is consequently a maximal $r$ such that the following diagram commutes.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.42\textwidth]{cd2-fig-07.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.7}{fig:cd2-07}
\end{figure}
The set $\lambda\overline{\mathbb D_r}$ is a compact subset of $\mathbb D_r$, and the image of $\mathbb D_r$ under multiplication by $\lambda$ lies in $\lambda\overline{\mathbb D_r}$. Thus there is a compact $K\subset U$ with $f(U)\subset K$, and
$f(\overline U)\subseteq\overline{f(U)}\subseteq K\subset U\subset\mathcal A$.
% Source page 12
By invariance, $\overline U\subset\mathcal A$, so $\phi$ is holomorphic on $\overline U$, since it extends to the whole basin. Let $w_0\in\partial\mathbb D_r$ and suppose $\partial U$ contains no critical point of $f$. Then $f$ is locally invertible at each $z_0\in\partial U$. But $\psi(w_0)$ may not yet be well-defined: the curve $t\mapsto\psi(tw_0)$ as $t\to1$ might accumulate at several points.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.78\textwidth]{cd2-fig-08.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.8}{fig:cd2-08}
\end{figure}
Let $z_0\in\partial U$ be an accumulation point of $\psi(tw_0)$ as $t\to1$. Since $f$ has a local inverse there, define, on a neighbourhood $V$ of $w_0$,
\[
\psi(w)=f^{-1}(\psi(\lambda w))\qquad(w\in V).
\]
Do this at every point. The identity theorem patches the local pieces over the compact set $\partial\mathbb D_r$, extending $\psi$ biholomorphically to a larger disc. That the extension is biholomorphic is again a consequence of the identity theorem.
\end{proof}
Thus critical points form the obstruction that stops, or contains, biholomorphic extension within a domain in $\mathcal A$.
% Source page 13

\setcounter{theorem}{13}\begin{theorem}\label{cd2:maximal-bottcher}
There is a maximal $0<r\le1$ such that the B\"ottcher coordinate $\phi$ has a biholomorphic inverse on $\mathbb D_r$. If $r=1$, then $\mathbb D\cong\mathcal A_0$, and the superattracting point $\widehat p$ is the only critical point in $\mathcal A_0$. Otherwise there is another critical point in $\mathcal A_0$, on $\partial\psi(\mathbb D_r)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
As before, a maximal $r\le1$ exists. For the following diagram to commute, the image of $\mathbb D_r$ under $z\mapsto z^n$, and all its subsequent iterates, must lie in $\mathbb D_r$, since $f^{\circ n}(\mathcal A_0)\subseteq\mathcal A_0$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.38\textwidth]{cd2-fig-09.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD2.9}{fig:cd2-09}
\end{figure}
Before, we had the luxury of the extended map $\phi$ on $\mathcal A$, so continuation of the holomorphic inverse by the identity theorem was a biholomorphic extension. We do not have that now, and must make do with the extension of $|\phi|$.

The map $\psi$ has no critical points in $\mathbb D_r$, since $\psi'(w)=0$ implies
$nw^{n-1}\psi'(w^n)=(f\circ\psi)'(w)=f'(\psi(w))\psi'(w)=0$.
Thus $w^n$, and all $w^{n^k}$, would be critical points, which is impossible by the identity theorem. Hence $\psi$ is locally injective (biholomorphic).

The set $\{(w_1,w_2)\in\mathbb D_r\times\mathbb D_r:\psi(w_1)=\psi(w_2)\}$ is closed. Thus choose $|w_1|=|w_2|$ minimal with $\psi(w_1)=\psi(w_2)$.
% Source page 14 Since $|\phi|$ extends to $\mathcal A$, we have $|\phi(\psi(w))|=|w|$ for all $w\in\mathbb D_\varepsilon$, the domain of biholomorphy. By the identity theorem, $|\phi(\psi(w))|=|w|$ for every $w\in\mathbb D_r$. Consequently $\psi(w_1)=\psi(w_2)$ implies $|w_1|=|w_2|$.

Let $w_1'\in B_\varepsilon(w_1)$. The set $\psi^{-1}(\psi(B_\varepsilon(w_1)))$ contains $w_2$, so $\psi(B_\varepsilon(w_1))\cap\psi(B_\varepsilon(w_2))\ne\varnothing$. There are therefore $w_1',w_2'$ with $|w_1'|<|w_1|$ and $\psi(w_1')=\psi(w_2')$, a contradiction. Thus $\psi$ is injective, hence biholomorphic onto $\psi(\mathbb D_r)$.

If $r=1$ and $\psi(\mathbb D)\subsetneq\mathcal A_0$, take $z_0\in\partial U\cap\mathcal A_0$. Approximate $z_0$ by $\psi(w_j)$. Then $|\phi(\psi(w_j))|=|w_j|\to1$, so $|\phi(z_0)|=1$, and $z_0$ does not tend to $0$, contradicting $z_0\in\mathcal A_0$.

If $r<1$, just as before, a critical point on the boundary prevents extension of $\psi$ beyond its maximal disc $\mathbb D_r$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
We defer the homeomorphism part to \BlogPost{parabolic-fixed-points-and-the-flower-theorem}{the next post}, where we explore topological properties, including continuous extension to $\partial\mathbb D_r$ of the inverses of B\"ottcher and Koenigs coordinates, and much more.
\end{remark}
\subsubsection{The number of attracting periodic points}
% Source page 15

\setcounter{theorem}{14}\begin{theorem}\label{cd2:finite-attractors}
There are finitely many attracting periodic orbits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For fixed points this follows because all immediate basins contain critical points, and a rational map has finitely many critical points. Given an attracting orbit $z_0\mapsto z_1\mapsto\cdots\mapsto z_0$ in $n$ steps, consider $f^{\circ n}$. The union of the immediate-basin components of the cycle contains a critical point of $f$, and distinct attracting cycles have disjoint immediate basins.
\end{proof}
\begin{proof}[Alternative proof]
Let $\widehat p\in O$ be an attracting fixed point, where $O$ is its immediate basin. If $O$ has no critical value of $f$, then there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $\widehat p$ and, for every $k\ge1$, a local inverse $g_k\colon U\to\widehat{\mathbb C}$ of $f^{\circ k}$. The family $\{g_k\}_k$ is normal, but $g_k'(\widehat p)$ is unbounded, since it is $\lambda^{-k}$ with $0<|\lambda|<1$.
\end{proof}
Critical points therefore bound the number of attracting periodic orbits. Let us try to extend this theorem to noncompact Riemann surfaces and nonrational maps.
\setcounter{definition}{8}\begin{definition}
For $f\colon S\to S'$, a \emph{critical value} is the image under $f$ of a critical point.
\end{definition}
\setcounter{definition}{9}\begin{definition}
If there is a curve $\gamma\colon[0,1)\to S$ that eventually leaves compact sets, but $\lim_{t\to1}f(\gamma(t))=v$, then $v$ is an \emph{asymptotic value}.
\end{definition}
% Source page 16

\setcounter{theorem}{15}\begin{theorem}
Every proper holomorphic self-map $f\colon S\to S$ has a critical value in every immediate basin of an attracting orbit.
\end{theorem}

Consider a fixed point $f(\widehat p)=\widehat p$ whose multiplier satisfies $\lambda^q=1$ and for which $f^{\circ q}$ is not locally the identity. Such points are called parabolic fixed points. It is immediately clear that the expected dynamics should be different. For instance, consider the orbits of $\pm1$ and $\pm i$ under $f(z)=z+z^3$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.55\textwidth]{cd3-fig-01.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD3.1}{fig:cd3-01}
\end{figure}
The map $f$ throws $1,-1$ violently away, while pulling $i,-i$ violently towards $\widehat p=0$. Notice that it pushes and pulls from two directions. The following theorem tells us that such patterns reappear. For now, no neighbourhood of $\widehat p=0$ can be expected to be a ``basin'', since $\widehat p$ is a sink only in certain directions.
% Source page 2

\setcounter{definition}{10}\begin{definition}
Let $f(z)=z+az^{n+1}+O(z^{n+2})$. A vector $v\in\mathbb C$ is attracting if $nav^n=-1$, and repelling if $nav^n=+1$.
\end{definition}
\begin{remark}
The equation $nav^n=\pm1$ has $2n$ equally spaced solutions. Also $f$ is locally invertible, with $f^{(n+1)}(0)\ne0$, $f'(0)=1$, and $f^{(k)}(0)=0$ for $1<k\le n$. Thus
\[
(f^{-1})''(0)=-\frac{f''(0)}{(f'(0))^3}=-2a\quad(n=1),
\]
and
\[
(f^{-1})'''(0)=\frac{3(f''(0))^2-f'(0)f'''(0)}{(f'(0))^5}
=-f'''(0)=-6a\quad(n=2).
\]
Inductively, $-f^{(n+1)}(0)=(f^{-1})^{(n+1)}(0)$. Thus attracting and repelling vectors exchange roles for $f^{-1}$.
\end{remark}
The next theorem explains why these vectors have their names, and shows that all maps with a parabolic point behave somewhat like $z+z^3$.
\setcounter{theorem}{16}\begin{theorem}\label{cd3:asymptotic}
If an orbit $\{z_k\}_k$ of $z_0$ tends to $0$ and $z_k\ne0$ for every $k$, then $z_k\sim v_j/k^{1/n}$ as $k\to\infty$ for some attracting vector $v_j$. The analogous assertion holds for orbits converging to $0$ under $f^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}
% Source page 3

We now know vaguely that there are domains in $\Delta_j$ around $\widehat p$ where $f$ pushes or pulls. We do not yet know whether those domains surround $\widehat p$; nothing so far prevents gaps. The domain $P_j(R)\subset\Delta_j$ satisfies $f(P_j(R))\subseteq P_j(R)$, and an orbit tending to $\widehat p$ along $v_j$ eventually enters it. Let us generalise this phenomenon.
\setcounter{definition}{12}\begin{definition}[Attracting and repelling petals]
Let $N$ be a neighbourhood of $\widehat p$ on which $f$ is univalent. A set $P\subset N$ is an attracting petal for $f$ at $\widehat p$, associated with an attracting vector $v_j$, if
\begin{itemize}
\item $f(P)\subseteq P$;
\item an orbit $\{z_k\}_k$ enters $P$ eventually if and only if $z_k\to\widehat p$ along $v_j$.
\end{itemize}
Repelling petals are defined similarly by considering $f^{-1}$ on $f(N)$.
\end{definition}
Clearly $P$ contains exactly one $v_j$; these sets therefore look like petals around the ``base branch'' $v_j$. The next theorem says that the attracting and repelling petals surround $\widehat p$: they fit together into a round flower.
% Source page 8

We now ask the question suggested by the case $0<|\lambda|<1$: can we understand the dynamics separately on each of the $2n$ petals? The next theorem addresses this completely.
\setcounter{theorem}{18}\begin{theorem}[Parabolic linearisation]\label{cd3:linearisation}
For any petal $P$, there is a conformal embedding $\alpha\colon P\to\mathbb C$, unique up to translation, making the following diagram commute. In the literature, $\alpha$ is called a \emph{Fatou coordinate}.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.34\textwidth]{cd3-fig-07.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD3.7}{fig:cd3-07}
\end{figure}
\end{theorem}
Before proving the theorem, let us make a few observations. Recall that $\Re F_j(w)>\Re w+1/2$ and
$|F_j(w)-w-1|=O(1/\sqrt[n]{|w|})$.
\begin{observation}[1]
For $\widehat w\in\mathbb H_R$, the maps $\beta_k(w)=F_j^{\circ k}(w)-F_j^{\circ k}(\widehat w)$ converge locally uniformly to a biholomorphic map.
\end{observation}
% Source page 11

Having observed these three crucial facts, let us prove existence and uniqueness of the Fatou coordinate.
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{cd3:linearisation}]
\emph{Existence.} For any petal $P$ and $z\in P$, some iterate enters $P_j(R)$. We have the following commutative diagram.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.65\textwidth]{cd3-fig-08.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD3.8}{fig:cd3-08}
\end{figure}
For $P=P_j(R)$, define $\alpha(z)=\beta(\varphi(z))$. For any $z\in P$, choose $k_0$ so that $f^{\circ k}(z)\in P_j(R)$ for all $k\ge k_0$, and define $\alpha(z)=\alpha(f^{\circ k}(z))-k$.
% Source page 15
This ensures that the following diagram also commutes.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.34\textwidth]{cd3-fig-09.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD3.9}{fig:cd3-09}
\end{figure}
\emph{Uniqueness.} Let $\alpha,\alpha'$ be two Fatou coordinates. Then $g=\alpha'\circ\alpha^{-1}$ commutes with $z\mapsto z+1$, so $g(w+1)=g(w)+1$. If $\alpha\colon P\to U$ and $\alpha'\colon P\to U'$ are biholomorphisms, then $g\colon U\to U'$ is a biholomorphism. Since the translates $U+n$ sweep out $\mathbb C$, extend $g$ to an entire function by integer translations, obtaining $\widehat g\colon\mathbb C\to U'+\mathbb Z$.

Its image misses at most one point. If $U'+\mathbb Z=\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$, without loss of generality, then $1\in U'+\mathbb Z$, so some integer $n$ belongs to $U'$. Hence $0\in U'-n\subseteq U'+\mathbb Z$, a contradiction. Thus $\widehat g(\mathbb C)=\mathbb C$ and $\widehat g\in\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb C)$. By the identity theorem, $\widehat g(w+1)=\widehat g(w)+1$.
Every automorphism of $\mathbb C$ is affine, and this identity forces $\widehat g(w)=w+b$.
% Source page 16
Therefore $\alpha'=\alpha+b$, so $\alpha$ is unique up to translation.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Critical points again play a crucial role; we will not elaborate on this. If $\alpha$ is the Fatou coordinate for the petal wrapping $v_j$, it extends to $\mathcal A_j(\widehat p)$, and $\alpha^{-1}$ extends to some $\mathbb H_R$ with $R$ optimal. This is analogous to the Koenigs-coordinate situation. For every $\mathcal A_j(\widehat p)$ there is a maximal petal $P_{\max}\subseteq\mathcal A_j(\widehat p)$ to which $\alpha$ extends biholomorphically, and $f$ has a critical point on $\partial P_{\max}$ that prevents further univalent extension.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
We have $\alpha(z)\to\infty$ as $z\to0$, and $0$ is a parabolic fixed point. The preimages $\{f^{-n}(0)\}$ form a dense subset of the basin boundary, obstructing expansion of $\alpha$.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
In conclusion, the $\mathcal A_j(\widehat p)$ are disjoint basins with $\partial\mathcal A_j(\widehat p)\subseteq J$. Each basin has a Fatou coordinate describing its local behaviour on a maximal petal $P_{\max}$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Periodic cycles}
In this short post we ask how many periodic points there are for a rational map $f=P/Q\colon\widehat{\mathbb C}\to\widehat{\mathbb C}$ with $\deg(f)=d\ge2$. It turns out that
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{0}\item the number of attracting periodic orbits is at most $2d-2$;
\setcounter{enumi}{1}\item the number of indifferent periodic orbits whose multiplier is not a root of unity is at most $4d-4$;
\setcounter{enumi}{2}\item there are infinitely many repelling periodic orbits.
\end{enumerate}
The first assertion is elementary: every immediate attracting basin has a critical point, and
\begin{align*}
\#\{\text{critical points of }P/Q\}
&=\#\{\text{zeros of }(P/Q)'\}\\
&=\#\left\{\text{zeros of }\frac{P'}Q-\frac{Q'P}{Q^2}\right\}
=\#\{\text{zeros of }QP'-Q'P\}\\
&\le\deg(QP'-Q'P)\le2d-2.
\end{align*}
Compare the leading coefficients in the worst case, $\deg P=\deg Q=d$. Now let us prove the second assertion.
\setcounter{theorem}{19}\begin{proposition}\label{cd4:indifferent}
The number of indifferent cycles whose multiplier is not a root of unity is at most $4d-4$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
We separate the proof into two stages.
\emph{Perturbation.} We will holomorphically deform $f(z)=P(z)/Q(z)$ into the polynomial $z^d$, the simplest rational map of degree $d$.
% Source page 2
We do this for two important reasons (revisit them):
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{0}\item If $\{z_j\}_j$ is an indifferent cycle, deform the $z_j$ holomorphically, along with their multipliers $\lambda_j$. We plan to convert them into attracting cycles and use the bound on attracting cycles.
\setcounter{enumi}{1}\item Deforming to $z^d$ helps ensure that this conversion happens for a ``large proportion'' of indifferent cycles.
\end{enumerate}
Define
\[
f_t(z)=\frac{P(z)-tz^d}{Q(z)-t},\qquad f_0(z)=f(z),\qquad f_\infty(z)=z^d.
\]
This remains a degree-$d$ map unless, for some $t\in\widehat{\mathbb C}$, zeros and poles collide as the parameter approaches $t$. For that to happen, $Q(z)=t$ and $P(z)=tz^d$, so $z^d=P(z)/Q(z)=f(z)$. Thus the number of solutions of $f(z)=z^d$ determines the number of exceptional parameters at which the degree drops.

Let $\{f_0^{\circ k}(z_j)\}_{k=1}^n$ be an $n$-cycle of $f_0$ with $|\lambda_j(0)|=1$. Set $\widetilde f(t,z)=f_t^{\circ n}(z)-z$. Since $\widetilde f(0,z_j)=0$, the implicit function theorem gives neighbourhoods $U$ of $0$ and $V$ of $z_j$ and a holomorphic map $z_j(t)\colon U\to V$ with $\widetilde f(t,z_j(t))=0$.
% Source page 3
The multiplier $\lambda_j(t)=(f_t^{\circ n})'(z_j(t))$ is also locally holomorphic. If it were locally constant, the identity theorem would shatter all hope of turning these indifferent points into attracting ones.
\setcounter{theorem}{20}\begin{lemma}\label{cd4:nonconstant}
The map $t\mapsto\lambda_j(t)$ is not locally constant.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose it is, and let us see how far this paralysis extends. Choose a ray $\{re^{i\theta}:0\le r\le+\infty\}$ avoiding every parameter where $\deg(f_t)<d$. Extend $\lambda_j(t)$ to a neighbourhood of this ray so that $f_t$ has an indifferent periodic point $z_j(t)$ with multiplier $\lambda_j(t)$.

If $z_j(t)\to z_j(\widehat t)$ along the ray as $t\to\widehat t$, continuity gives
$\widetilde f(t,z_j(t))\to\widetilde f(\widehat t,z_j(\widehat t))=0$.
The implicit function theorem makes $z_j$, and hence $\lambda_j$, analytic near $\widehat t$. Thus the domain of analytic continuation is both open and closed. It follows that $z^d$ has an indifferent cycle $\{f_\infty^{\circ k}(z_j(\infty))\}_k$ with $|\lambda_j(\infty)|=1$. This is impossible: the periodic points of $z^d$ are $0,\infty$,
% Source page 4
and roots of unity, none of whose multipliers lie on $S^1$.
\end{proof}
There is therefore hope: the functions $\lambda_j(t)$ are not paralysed; they are indeed ``dynamic''.

\emph{Converting cycles.} Near $t=0$, write
\[
\frac{\lambda_j(t)}{\lambda_j(0)}=1+a_jt^{n_j}+o(t^{n_j}),\qquad
|\lambda_j(t)|=1+\Re(a_jt^{n_j})+o(t^{n_j}).
\]
Set $\sigma_j(\theta)=\operatorname{sign}\Re(a_je^{i\theta n_j})$. For small $r$, $\sigma_j(\theta)=+1$ implies $|\lambda_j(re^{i\theta})|>1$, while $\sigma_j(\theta)=-1$ implies $|\lambda_j(re^{i\theta})|<1$. This is the desired conversion.

Clearly $(2\pi)^{-1}\int_0^{2\pi}\sigma_j(\theta)\,d\theta=0$. Let $\ell$ be odd and no greater than the number of indifferent cycles of $f_0$. Then
\[
\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\sum_{k=1}^{\ell}\sigma_k(\theta)\,d\theta=0.
\]
For some $\theta$, at least $(\ell+1)/2$ multipliers have modulus less than one. Hence, for $t=re^{i\theta}$, the map $f_t$ has at least $(\ell+1)/2$ attracting cycles. Since $(\ell+1)/2\le2d-2$, the number of indifferent cycles is finite and at most $4d-4$.
\end{proof}
% Source page 5

In this post we see how the critical points of polynomials on $\widehat{\mathbb C}$ determine the shape and structure of the Julia set. The following is a well-known fact.
\setcounter{theorem}{22}\begin{theorem}\label{cd5:components}
If $\mathcal A_0$ is an immediate basin of attraction, then $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal A_0$ is either connected or has uncountably many components.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $N_0$ small enough that $f(\overline{N_0})\subset N_0$ and $\partial N_0$ contains no forward image of a critical point. Such images are at most countable, because $\{z:f'(z)=0\}$ is finite. Let $\widehat z$ be the fixed point, and let $N_k$ be the component of $f^{-k}(N_0)$ containing $\widehat z$.

By invariance, $N_0\subset N_1\subset N_2\subset\cdots$. The iterates converge locally uniformly on compact sets. Given a path $P$ from $\widehat z$ to any $z\in\mathcal A_0$, we have $f^{\circ k}(P)\subseteq N_0$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Thus $z\in f^{-k}(N_0)$, and $\bigcup_kN_k=\mathcal A_0$.
% Source page 2
Therefore $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal A_0=\bigcap_k(\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus N_k)$. If each $\partial N_k$ is connected, being a Jordan curve, and has no components, then $\{\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus N_k\}_k$ is a nested sequence of compact connected sets. Hence $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal A_0$ is connected.

Otherwise, $\overline{N_k}$ is a $d$-fold branched covering of $\overline{N_{k-1}}$, and the same holds for their components: if $C$ and $D$ are such components, $f|_C\colon C\to D$ is a $d$-fold branched covering. Thus
\[
f\colon\overline{N_k}\setminus f^{-1}(N_{k-1})\longrightarrow
\overline{N_{k-1}}\setminus N_{k-1}
\]
is also a $d$-fold branched covering. The following figure completes the proof. Since $f$ is conformal on $\partial N_k$, it sends the interior of $\partial N_k$ to the interior of $f(\partial N_k)$, preserving angles and therefore direction.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.65\textwidth]{cd5-fig-01.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD5.1}{fig:cd5-01}
\end{figure}
The union of intersections of these Jordan-curve interiors forms $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal A_0$, which is evidently uncountable.
\end{proof}
% Source page 3

We now ask when $\widehat{\mathbb C}\setminus\mathcal A_0$ is connected. This reveals the topological shape of polynomial Julia sets. Let us see the role of critical points, working in the ambient plane $\mathbb C\subset\widehat{\mathbb C}$.
\setcounter{definition}{13}\begin{definition}[Filled Julia set]
The filled Julia set is $K=\{z\in\mathbb C:\text{the orbit of }z\text{ is bounded}\}$.
\end{definition}
\subsection{Description of the filled Julia set}
For $f(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{d}a_kz^k$ of degree $d\ge2$, first normalise by $cf(z/c)$ to make $f$ monic. There is $r$ with $B_r(\infty)\subseteq\mathcal A(\infty)$, so the filled Julia set is $\mathcal A(\infty)^c$. Recall that if $J$ has interior, then $J=\widehat{\mathbb C}$, which is not the case here. Thus $J=\partial K$, and all bounded Fatou components belong to $\operatorname{Int}(K)$.

For example, a crosscut $A$ joins boundary points $x,y$. Since $U\cong\mathbb D$, for every sequence $x_i\in U$ tending to $\partial U$, the sequence $\varphi(x_i)$ accumulates on $\partial\mathbb D$. The image $\varphi(A)$ is a crosscut of $\mathbb D$. By the Jordan curve theorem it separates $\mathbb D$ into two components, and pulling these components back by $\varphi$ separates $U$ into two crosscut neighbourhoods $U^+$ and $U^-$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.51\textwidth]{cd8-fig-02.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD8.2}{fig:cd8-02}
\end{figure}
The signs depend on the orientation of $A$.

To prevent failure of boundary continuity of $\varphi$ or $\varphi^{-1}$, use nested crosscut neighbourhoods around boundary points: a fundamental chain $\mathcal N=\{N_j\}_j$ of crosscut neighbourhoods with $N_1\supset N_2\supset\cdots$. If $A_j$ are the corresponding crosscuts, require $\overline A_j\cap\overline A_i=\varnothing$ for $i\ne j$, and $\diam(\overline A_j)\to0$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.52\textwidth]{cd8-fig-03.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD8.3}{fig:cd8-03}
\end{figure}
Combining measure-theoretic and topological arguments, using $\mathbb H^+$ as the universal cover of $\mathbb D\setminus\{0\}$, Carath\'eodory shows that fundamental chains are a topological invariant. This makes the following natural.
\setcounter{definition}{19}\begin{definition}[Carath\'eodory compactification]
Two fundamental chains $\{N_j\}$ and $\{N_j'\}$ are equivalent if each eventually lies in every member of the other: for every $k$ there are $j,j'$ such that $N_j\subset N_k'$ and $N_{j'}'\subset N_k$. An equivalence class $\mathcal E$ is called a prime end of $U$. The Carath\'eodory compactification is
\[
\widehat U=U\sqcup\{\mathcal E:\mathcal E\text{ is a prime end of }U\}.
\]
\end{definition}
% Source page 4

\setcounter{theorem}{34}\begin{theorem}[Landing criterion]\label{cd8:landing}
The following are equivalent:
\begin{itemize}
\item every ray $R_t$, $t\in\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$, lands, and $\gamma(t)$ is continuous;
\item $J=\partial K$ is locally connected;
\item $K$ is locally connected;
\item $\phi^{-1}\colon\mathbb C\setminus\overline{\mathbb D}\to\mathbb C\setminus K$ extends continuously to the boundary.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Carath\'eodory's theorem.
\end{proof}
We offer a slightly deeper insight than the landing criterion.
\setcounter{theorem}{35}\begin{theorem}[Rational rays land]\label{cd8:rational-rays}
Every rational external ray lands. A periodic ray lands at a repelling or parabolic periodic point, while a strictly preperiodic ray lands at a preperiodic point.
\end{theorem}
Before proving this, we need a preparatory lemma that is significant in its own right.
\setcounter{theorem}{36}\begin{lemma}[Snail lemma]
Suppose $p\colon[0,\infty)\to V\setminus\{0\}$, $p(t)\to0$ as $t\to\infty$, and $f(0)=0$, with $f(z)=\lambda z+a_2z^2+\cdots$ on $V$. Then
\begin{itemize}
\item $f(p(t))=p(t+1)$ for every $t\ge0$ implies $|\lambda|<1$ or $\lambda=1$;
\item $f(p(t))=p(t-1)$ for every $t\ge1$ implies $|\lambda|>1$ or $\lambda=1$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}
% Source page 6

\setcounter{theorem}{37}\begin{lemma}[Preparatory lemma]\label{cd8:preparatory}
If $R_t=f(R_t)$ lands at $z_0$, then $z_0$ is a repelling or parabolic fixed point.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Parametrise the ray by $p\colon\mathbb R\to\mathbb C\setminus K$, where $p(s)=z$ is determined by $\log\log|\phi(z)|=s$. This $z$ is unique: the argument is fixed by $R_t$, and the modulus is fixed by $|\phi(z)|=e^{e^s}$. Also $z_0=\gamma(t)=\lim_{s\to-\infty}p(s)$.

Since $\phi(f(z))=\phi(z)^d$, we have $\log|\phi(f(z))|=d\log|\phi(z)|$, and $\log\log|\phi(f(z))|=\log d+\log\log|\phi(z)|$. Therefore $f(p(s))=p(s+\log d)$. The landing point is fixed, and the snail lemma gives $|\lambda|>1$ or $\lambda=1$.
\end{proof}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{cd8:rational-rays}]
First suppose that $t$ is periodic under multiplication by $d$. Choose $q\ge1$ with $d^qt\equiv t\pmod1$, and put
\[
g=f^{\circ q},\qquad D=d^q.
\]
Then $g(R_t)=R_t$. We must return to hyperbolic geometry to go beyond the spherical landing criterion. Give $\mathbb C\setminus K$ its Poincar\'e metric using $\mathbb C\setminus K\cong\mathbb C\setminus\overline{\mathbb D}$. The map $\psi\colon\mathbb H^+\to\mathbb C\setminus\overline{\mathbb D}$, $\psi(w)=e^w$, is a covering, and $z\mapsto z^D$ lifts to the automorphism $w\mapsto Dw$ of $\mathbb H^+$. Hence $g$ is a local isometry for the Poincar\'e metric.
% Source page 7
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.54\textwidth]{cd8-fig-04.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD8.4}{fig:cd8-04}
\end{figure}
Recall the parametrisation $g(p(s))=p(s+\log D)$. Then
\[
R_t=p(\mathbb R)=\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb Z}I_k
=\bigcup_{k\in\mathbb Z}p([k\log D,(k+1)\log D]),
\]
with $g(I_k)=I_{k+1}$. All the segments $I_k$ have the same finite Poincar\'e length. Also $G(p(s))=e^s\to0$ as $s\to-\infty$. Thus any limit point $\widehat z$ of $p(s)$ has $\log|\phi(\widehat z)|=0$, so $\phi(\widehat z)\in\partial\mathbb D$ and $\widehat z\in\partial K=J$. Such a limit point exists because $\overline{\mathbb C\setminus K}$ is compact in $\widehat{\mathbb C}$. The limit-point configuration is illustrated below; once fixedness is established, the preparatory lemma applies.

As $k\to-\infty$, the segments $I_k$ approach the boundary $J$; the comparison between the Poincar\'e metric and the spherical distance to the boundary therefore gives
\[
\operatorname{diam}_{\mathrm{sph}}(I_k)\longrightarrow0.
\]
Let
\[
L_t=\bigcap_{s\in\mathbb R}\overline{p(( -\infty,s])}
\]
be the cluster set of the ray at the Julia set. It is nonempty, compact, and connected. For a neighbourhood $N$ of $\widehat z\in L_t$, there is a smaller neighbourhood $N'\subset N$ containing an entire $I_k$, since the Poincar\'e length of every $I_k$ is $\log D$ while its spherical diameter tends to zero. Hence $N\cap g(N)\ne\varnothing$, as $g$ maps one endpoint of $I_k$ to the other. Equivalently, if a sequence of segments $I_{k_j}$ approaches $\widehat z$, both endpoints tend to $\widehat z$, and continuity gives $g(\widehat z)=\widehat z$. Thus
\[
L_t\subseteq\operatorname{Fix}(g).
\]
The fixed-point set of $g$ is finite, whereas $L_t$ is connected, so $L_t$ is a single point. Therefore $R_t$ lands. The preparatory lemma, applied to $g$, shows that the landing point is repelling or parabolic for $g$, and hence periodic for $f$.
\begin{figure}[H]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.65\textwidth]{cd8-fig-05.pdf}
\FigureTag{CD8.5}{fig:cd8-05}
\end{figure}

Now let $t$ be rational but not periodic. Some angle $d^mt$ is periodic, so the ray
\[
f^{\circ m}(R_t)=R_{d^mt}
\]
lands at a periodic point $y$. The cluster set $L_t$ is connected and satisfies
\[
f^{\circ m}(L_t)=\{y\}.
\]
Hence $L_t$ is contained in the finite set $(f^{\circ m})^{-1}(y)$. A connected subset of a finite set is a single point, so $R_t$ lands at a preperiodic point.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
We have $g(I_k)=I_{k+1}$. A holomorphic map on a hyperbolic surface is distance-reducing unless it is a covering map. Here $z\mapsto z^D$ on $\mathbb C\setminus\overline{\mathbb D}$ is a covering, so $g$ is a local isometry. Since $g|_{I_k}$ is injective, it maps $I_k$ isometrically to $I_{k+1}$.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
One can also prove the converse of Theorem~\ref{cd8:rational-rays}.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
The classification of hyperbolic self-maps, together with Sullivan's no-wandering theorem and the snail lemma, gives Sullivan's classification of Fatou components for rational maps.
\end{remark}

